+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Is South Carolina a 50/50 divorce state?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. South Carolina law has no equal-split rule. The judge weighs 15 factors to divide marital property, and the split is not always half."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where does the money from the sale go until the divorce is settled?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The money is paid as your agreement or the court's order says. If you disagree about part of it, the lawyer holding it keeps that part separate until you agree or a court decides. The rule has one narrow exception."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do we need an appraisal for the divorce?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not always. The two of you can agree on a value, from an appraisal or an agent's written price estimate, which may not be called an appraisal. If you do not agree, the judge decides the value and may appoint an expert, such as an appraiser."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who pays the mortgage during the divorce?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Both of you still owe the lender while both names are on the loan. Who makes the payments while the case is open is a question for a family law attorney."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is a house I inherited marital property in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually not. What one spouse inherited is not marital property under the law. Whether it became marital, for example after it was put in both names, is a question for a family law attorney."}}]}</script>
