1.8"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/></svg></span><span><strong>$0 deed fee</strong><span class="t">on a buyout deed between spouses; a $350,000 sale pays $1,295</span></span></li><li><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 3h8l4 4v13a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H6a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V4a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM14 3v4h4M9 17l6-6"/><circle cx="9.5" cy="11.5" r="1"/><circle cx="14.5" cy="16.5" r="1"/></svg></span><span><strong>Under $250,000</strong><span class="t">the estimated gain on a typical Horry County house bought in 1998 or later</span></span></li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 8, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">If the two owners cannot agree on the house during a divorce, South Carolina's Family Court decides, and it can order the house sold.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. You can sell a house during a divorce in South Carolina, before or after the divorce is final, when the two of you agree. If you do not, the Family Court decides and can order the house sold.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The court usually treats the house as marital property, even if only one spouse's name is on the deed. If a spouse will not sign a deed the judge orders, the clerk of court can.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Take the middle Horry County home, condos aside: about $350,000, with an example $200,000 owed. Sold and split equally, it leaves each of you about $74,350 before agent fees and other closing costs. In a buyout, the spouse who keeps it pays the other $75,000.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">A written price estimate for the house that both of you can use, free.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Have us price the house for both of you</a></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory);padding-top:2.5rem"><div class="wrap"><nav aria-label="On this page" style="margin-top:0;max-width:720px"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.3rem;font-weight:600">On this page</p><ul style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0"><li><a href="#who-gets-it" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Who gets the house</a></li><li><a href="#choices" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">The three choices</a></li><li><a href="#buyout" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">How a buyout works</a></li><li><a href="#agree" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">If you do not agree</a></li><li><a href="#tax" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Tax and timing</a></li><li><a href="#steps" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">The steps</a></li><li><a href="#selling-a-house-in-a-divorce-faq" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Selling a house in a divorce: FAQ</a></li></ul></nav></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="who-gets-it"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Who gets the house">Who gets the house in a divorce in South Carolina?</h2><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}.c3ph-gal{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:1.5rem;margin:1.6rem 0;padding:0;list-style:none}.c3ph-gal>figure{margin:0;min-width:0}.c3ph-gal img{aspect-ratio:var(--c3ph-r,4/3)}.c3ph-gal figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.45;margin-top:.5rem}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-gal{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(var(--c3ph-n,3),minmax(0,1fr));gap:1rem;overflow:visible;padding:0}.c3ph-gal>figure{min-width:0}}@media (max-width:699px){.c3ph-gal.c3ph-row{display:flex;gap:.75rem;padding:0 var(--c3ph-bleed,1.4rem) .4rem;margin:1.6rem calc(-1*var(--c3ph-bleed,1.4rem)) 0;overflow-x:auto;scroll-snap-type:x mandatory;scroll-padding:0 var(--c3ph-bleed,1.4rem);-webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch;scrollbar-width:none}.c3ph-row::-webkit-scrollbar{display:none}.c3ph-row>figure{flex:0 0 82%;scroll-snap-align:start}}.c3ph-swipe{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.75rem;margin:.5rem 0 1.6rem;color:var(--navy);font:500 .95rem/1.3 var(--sans)}.c3ph-dots{display:inline-flex;gap:.45rem}.c3ph-dots i{display:block;width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;border:1.5px solid var(--navy);box-sizing:border-box}.c3ph-dots i:first-child{background:var(--navy)}@supports (animation-timeline:view()){.c3ph-rowbox{timeline-scope:--c3ph-p1,--c3ph-p2,--c3ph-p3}.c3ph-row>figure:nth-child(1){view-timeline:--c3ph-p1 inline}.c3ph-row>figure:nth-child(2){view-timeline:--c3ph-p2 inline}.c3ph-row>figure:nth-child(3){view-timeline:--c3ph-p3 inline}.c3ph-dots i{animation:c3ph-dot linear both;animation-range:cover}.c3ph-dots i:nth-child(1){animation-timeline:--c3ph-p1}.c3ph-dots i:nth-child(2){animation-timeline:--c3ph-p2}.c3ph-dots i:nth-child(3){animation-timeline:--c3ph-p3}@keyframes c3ph-dot{0%,36%{background:transparent}42%,60%{background:var(--navy)}66%,100%{background:transparent}}}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-swipe{display:none}}.c3ph-cards{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.75rem;margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;padding:0;list-style:none}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-cards{grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:1rem}}@media (min-width:1000px){.c3ph-cards.c3ph-4{grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr))}}.c3ph-card{margin:0;background:var(--ivory);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;display:flex;flex-direction:column}.c3ph-card .c3ph-f{border-radius:0}.c3ph-card img,.c3ph-card svg{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;aspect-ratio:var(--c3ph-r,4/3);object-fit:cover}.c3ph-card .c3ph-cr{font-size:.56rem;padding:1.2rem .45rem .25rem 1.6rem}.c3ph-card strong{display:block;color:var(--navy);font-size:.98rem;font-weight:500;line-height:1.3;padding:.7rem .8rem 0}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{display:block;color:var(--muted);font-size:.86rem;line-height:1.45;padding:.2rem .8rem .85rem}.c3ph-credits{margin:2rem 0 1rem;padding:1rem 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.55;max-width:760px}.c3ph-credits .c3ph-h{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .4rem}.c3ph-credits [role=listitem]{margin:0 0 .3rem}.c3ph-credits a{color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(28,32,40,.3);text-underline-offset:2px}</style><style>section[style*="background:var(--ivory)"] .c3ph-card{background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-cards{max-width:920px}.c3ph-card strong{font-family:var(--sans)}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{font-size:1rem!important}.c3ph-card strong{font-size:1rem!important}ul[role=list] li span[style*="font-size:.9rem"]{font-size:1rem!important}section#c3-photo{padding-top:0}section:has(+section#c3-photo){background:var(--ivory-2)!important;padding-bottom:2rem}.c3ph-hero{margin:0}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero{max-width:900px;margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:auto!important;height:auto}.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:640px}.c3-aerial-ctop .c3ph-cr{top:0;bottom:auto;padding:.35rem .6rem 1.6rem 2.6rem;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 0%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0))}@media (max-width:699px){.c3ph-cards>:last-child:nth-child(odd){grid-column:1/-1}}.c3-maps{display:grid;gap:0 1.5rem;max-width:1136px}@media (min-width:900px){.c3-maps{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;align-items:start}}.c3-maps figure{max-width:none!important;margin:1.4rem 0 .4rem!important}.c3-maps svg,.c3-maps img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}.c3-map-links{row-gap:0!important}.c3-map-links a{padding:.55rem 0}ul[role=list][style*="minmax(13rem,1fr)"]{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(min(13rem,100%),1fr))!important}</style><style>.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero,.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:720px}</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=63&amp;chapter=3" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Family Court</a> divides the house as part of the marital property, and the split is not always half. South Carolina's <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=20&amp;chapter=3" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">law</a> calls this equitable apportionment, meaning a fair split; most people say equitable distribution.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In South Carolina, a house bought during the marriage is usually marital property, even if only one spouse's name is on the deed. The exceptions include a house bought with one spouse's inherited or premarital money, or one a prenuptial agreement leaves out.</p><div class="dv-two" role="group" aria-label="What South Carolina divides in a divorce, and what it does not"><div><p class="k">Usually divided</p><ul><li>A house bought during the marriage, even if only one spouse's name is on the deed</li><li>Anything else either of you bought during the marriage and still owns on the day the case is filed</li><li>A rise in the value of one spouse's house that came from the other spouse's efforts</li></ul></div><div><p class="k">Not divided</p><ul><li>What one spouse inherited, or was given by someone other than the other spouse</li><li>What one spouse owned before the marriage, and what was bought with either of those</li><li>What a written agreement, such as a prenuptial agreement, leaves out</li><li>What is bought after a temporary court order or a signed property agreement</li></ul></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A house one spouse owned before the marriage is not marital property under the law. Any rise in its value that came from the other spouse's efforts can be divided. Whether an inherited or premarital house became marital is a question for a family law attorney. That includes a house later put in both names or paid off with joint money.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is it split 50/50?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. South Carolina law does not split marital property in half by rule. The judge weighs 15 factors, as much or as little as the judge finds right.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The factors include how long the marriage lasted and each spouse's income. They include each spouse's contributions, a homemaker's included, any debts on the property and the tax effect of the split. One factor is whether the parent with custody of the children gets the family home, or the right to live in it for a reasonable time.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Once the court divides the property, that part of the order is final. It can be changed only on appeal.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="choices"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The three choices">Sell, buy out your spouse, or keep the house: what does each leave you?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The two of you can choose any of the three by agreement. If you cannot agree, the judge chooses, and can order the house sold at a public or a private sale.</p><ul class="dv-cards" aria-label="Three choices for the house in a divorce"><li><span class="k">Choice 1</span><b>Sell and split</b>The house is sold, the loan is paid off at closing, and the two of you divide what is left. The sale pays the deed fee and agent fees. Neither of you owes the old loan after.</li><li><span class="k">Choice 2</span><b>One buys the other out</b>One of you keeps the house and pays the other for their share. The deed between you pays no deed fee, and there is no agent fee. The money can come from a <a href="#buyout" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">new loan</a>.</li><li><span class="k">Choice 3</span><b>One keeps it for a time</b>One of you stays in the house for a time, and it is sold or bought out later. Both of you still owe the loan, unless the lender releases one of you.</li></ul><div class="dv-side"><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The table works out the first two choices on the county's middle home, condos aside. The $200,000 still owed is an example, not a typical balance. An equal split is one example too, since the court's split is not always equal.</p><div class="dv-calc" role="group" aria-labelledby="dvCalcT"><p class="k" id="dvCalcT">Try your own numbers</p><div class="dv-in"><label for="dvP">Value of the house</label><span class="u">$ <input id="dvP" type="number" min="0" step="1000" value="350000" oninput="dvCalc()"></span></div><div class="dv-in"><label for="dvO">Still owed on it</label><span class="u">$ <input id="dvO" type="number" min="0" step="1000" value="200000" oninput="dvCalc()"></span></div><div class="dv-in"><label for="dvF">Agent fees, in percent</label><span class="u"><input id="dvF" class="sm" type="number" min="0" max="10" step="0.25" value="1" oninput="dvCalc()"> %</span></div><div class="dv-in"><label for="dvS">Your share, the split you agree or the court orders</label><span class="u"><input id="dvS" class="sm" type="number" min="0" max="100" step="1" value="50" oninput="dvCalc()"> %</span></div><dl class="dv-out"><div><dt>If you sell, you get about</dt><dd id="dvR1">$72,603</dd></div><div><dt>If your spouse keeps it, you are paid</dt><dd id="dvR2">$75,000</dd></div><div><dt>If you keep it, you pay your spouse</dt><dd id="dvR3">$75,000</dd></div><div><dt>and your new loan is at least</dt><dd id="dvR4">$275,000</dd></div></dl><p class="n">The fee box starts at 1 percent, the unit this page uses. Closing attorney's fees and the new loan's own costs are not counted.</p></div><script>function dvCalc(){var g=function(i){var v=parseFloat(document.getElementById(i).value);return isFinite(v)&&v>0?v:0};var m=function(n){return "$"+Math.round(Math.max(0,n)).toLocaleString("en-US")};var p=g("dvP"),o=g("dvO"),f=g("dvF"),s=Math.min(g("dvS"),100)/100;var e=p-o,d=Math.ceil(p/500)*1.85;document.getElementById("dvR1").textContent=m((e-d-p*f/100)*s);document.getElementById("dvR2").textContent=m(e*s);document.getElementById("dvR3").textContent=m(e*(1-s));document.getElementById("dvR4").textContent=m(o+e*(1-s))}</script><table class="dv-tab"><caption>Example: the middle home at $350,000, with $200,000 still owed</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col"><span class="sr">Item</span></th><th scope="col">Sell and split</th><th scope="col">One buys the other out</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Equity, the value less what is owed</th><td>$150,000</td><td>$150,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Deed fee</th><td>$1,295</td><td>$0</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Agent fees</th><td>$3,500 for each 1 percent</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Each of you, if split equally</th><td>About $74,350, less $1,750 for each 1 percent of agent fees</td><td>$75,000 to the spouse who leaves</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">New loan for the one who keeps it</th><td>None</td><td>At least $275,000</td></tr></tbody></table><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Both columns are before the closing attorney's fee and other closing costs. Our <a href="/sell/net-proceeds/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">net proceeds calculator</a> counts every cost of a sale, and our <a href="/sell/home-value/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">home value page</a> shows how a house here is priced from recorded sales.</p></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If one of you keeps the house for a time, the money question waits until it is sold or bought out. A written agreement or order that lets one spouse live there also matters for the <a href="#tax" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">tax rule</a> later.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">What would each choice leave the two of you?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send us the address and what is still owed. We price the house from recorded sales near it and set a sale and a buyout side by side.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us work out both choices on your house</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="buyout"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="How a buyout works">How does a buyout work, and how does the other spouse get off the mortgage?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In a buyout, the spouse who keeps the house pays the other for their share. A buyout can be paid with a new loan, which also pays off the old one.</p><ol class="dv-num" aria-label="How a buyout works, in order"><li><b>Agree on the value</b>Use an appraisal, or a real estate agent's written price estimate. Our agents check recorded sales near the house before they write one. An agent's estimate <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=40&amp;chapter=60" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">may not be called an appraisal</a>, and it cannot be used to get a federally related loan. If you do not agree, the judge decides the value from the evidence. The judge may appoint an expert, such as an appraiser, and charge the cost to either or both of you.</li><li><b>Write the terms down</b>The agreement says who keeps the house, what the other is paid and by when. For a loan Fannie Mae buys, all owners sign a written agreement on the transfer and where the money goes.</li><li><b>The new loan</b>For a loan Fannie Mae buys, a refinance that pays one owner for their share because of a divorce has <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b2-1.3-02/limited-cash-out-refinance-transactions" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">its own rules</a>. It counts as a limited cash-out refinance only if the two of you owned the house together for at least 12 months before the new loan pays out. The spouse who keeps the house gets none of the cash and must qualify for the new loan under Fannie Mae's rules. Other loan types have their own rules.</li><li><b>The deed</b>A deed from one spouse to the other, or to a former spouse as part of the divorce, is <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=12&amp;chapter=24" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">exempt from the deed fee</a>. The exception is a spouse receiving it who is a nonresident alien, a tax term for someone who is neither a US citizen nor a US resident. The deed is filed with a short statement giving the reason it is exempt. "Part of the divorce" means within 1 year after the marriage ends, or under the divorce or separation papers within 6 years.</li><li><b>The old loan is paid off</b>The new loan pays off the old one. That ends the debt of the spouse who left.</li></ol><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Signing the house over, with a quitclaim deed or any other deed, changes who owns it. It does not take anyone off the loan.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">If the old loan stays</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A home of one to four units can pass to the borrower's spouse under a divorce decree or property settlement. If the borrower lived there and the spouse who gets it lives there or will, the lender <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/12/1701j-3" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">cannot demand the whole loan be paid</a> for that. The loan can stay in place. A rental or a second home is not covered.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">That does not take the other spouse off the loan. A divorce order that gives the house and its loan to one spouse does not by itself release the other. Unless the lender releases them, they still owe it.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A spouse still on the old loan may apply for a new home loan. A lender following <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b3-6-05/monthly-debt-obligations" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae's rules</a> does not have to count that debt, if a court order assigned it to the ex-spouse. Our <a href="/homes/map/?type=house" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">map of houses for sale</a> shows what is listed in Horry County now, and our page on <a href="/sell/sell-or-buy-first/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">selling or buying first</a> covers the order of the two.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="agree"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="If you do not agree">Can my spouse sell the house without me, or refuse to sell?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A spouse who refuses to sell does not end the matter. The judge can order a spouse to sign the deed. If that spouse will not, the judge can <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=20&amp;chapter=3" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">have the county's clerk of court sign it</a> instead, and that signature counts the same.</p><h3 id="notice" style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">A house in one spouse's name</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A house in one spouse's name can still be marital property. Either spouse can file a <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=15&amp;chapter=11" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">notice of the divorce case</a> with the clerk of court in the county where the house is. Lawyers call the notice a lis pendens.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Until it is filed, a spouse's claim to a house in the other spouse's name does not bind a buyer or lender. After it is filed, a buyer or lender who takes an interest later is bound by the case. It does not affect a lender or buyer whose rights came first. Whether to file one is a question for a family law attorney.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">If the deed says joint tenants with rights of survivorship</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Some deeds name just the two spouses as "joint tenants with rights of survivorship". While you are joint tenants, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=27&amp;chapter=7" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">one of you cannot mortgage the house</a> without the other. Until the final divorce order is filed, if one of you dies, the other owns the whole house.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our agents read the deed before we list, so we know how the two of you own the house. The final divorce order ends that joint tenancy, unless the court's order says otherwise. Each of you then owns a share as a tenant in common, so neither takes the other's share at death.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How a listing works when the two of you do not agree</h3><ul class="dv-plain" aria-label="How a listing works for two spouses"><li><b>Both of you sign the listing</b>An agent may not market a house without a written listing agreement with the owner. When a husband and wife own the house together, we have both sign the listing.</li><li><b>Every offer in writing, to both of you</b>The agent puts every offer in writing and presents it to the seller promptly. We send each offer to both of you at the same time.</li><li><b>Every change initialed</b>Changes made during negotiations are in writing, initialed and dated by both sides.</li><li><b>The same duties to each of you</b>A listing firm owes its client loyalty, obedience, disclosure, confidentiality, reasonable care, diligence and accounting. When two spouses sell, both are our client, so we tell both of you the same thing.</li></ul><h3 id="mediation" style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">If you cannot agree on a price or an offer</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most contested issues in a divorce case, the house included, <a href="https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/adr/rule-3/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">must go to mediation</a>. The exceptions are a case that is exempt, or spouses who agree to arbitration or early neutral evaluation instead. A mediator is a neutral person who helps the two sides talk. The mediator does not decide, and any agreement is voluntary.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If the two of you still do not agree, the judge decides, and can order the house sold on terms the judge decides.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Selling while the two of you are apart?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Both of you sign, both of you get every offer, and every change is in writing. Send us the address and who is on the deed.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us list it with both of you</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="tax"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Tax and timing">Capital gains tax in a divorce: should you sell before or after it is final?</h2><div class="dv-side"><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a typical Horry County house bought in 1998 or later, the estimated whole gain is under one person's $250,000 limit. For one bought in the mid-1990s it is close to the limit. Which limit each of you can use depends on the dates of the sale and the divorce.</p><figure class="dv-chart"><p class="cap" id="dvChartT">Estimated gain on a typical Horry County house that sells for about $350,000 today, by the year it was bought</p><div class="dv-plot" id="dvPlot"><span class="dv-yl" style="top:100%">$0</span><span class="dv-yl" style="top:66.66666666666667%">$100k</span><span class="dv-yl" style="top:33.333333333333336%">$200k</span><svg viewBox="0 0 600 240" preserveAspectRatio="none" role="img" aria-label="Estimated gain on a typical Horry County house that sells for about $350,000 today, by the year it was bought: close to the $250,000 limit for a house bought in the mid-1990s, about $151,000 for 2006, $183,000 for 2016 and $120,000 for 2021, falling toward zero for a house bought recently."><line x1="0" x2="600" y1="240" y2="240" class="g" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"/><line x1="0" x2="600" y1="160" y2="160" class="g" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"/><line x1="0" x2="600" y1="80" y2="80" class="g" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"/><polygon points="0,240 0.0,35.2 20.0,40.8 40.0,43.2 60.0,49.6 80.0,52.0 100.0,55.2 120.0,60.0 140.0,64.0 160.0,67.2 180.0,74.4 200.0,89.6 220.0,119.2 240.0,131.2 260.0,123.2 280.0,112.8 300.0,92.0 320.0,81.6 340.0,77.6 360.0,78.4 380.0,85.6 400.0,90.4 420.0,93.6 440.0,100.8 460.0,106.4 480.0,116.8 500.0,123.2 520.0,144.0 540.0,191.2 560.0,213.6 580.0,224.0 600.0,237.6 600,240" class="a"/><polyline points="0.0,35.2 20.0,40.8 40.0,43.2 60.0,49.6 80.0,52.0 100.0,55.2 120.0,60.0 140.0,64.0 160.0,67.2 180.0,74.4 200.0,89.6 220.0,119.2 240.0,131.2 260.0,123.2 280.0,112.8 300.0,92.0 320.0,81.6 340.0,77.6 360.0,78.4 380.0,85.6 400.0,90.4 420.0,93.6 440.0,100.8 460.0,106.4 480.0,116.8 500.0,123.2 520.0,144.0 540.0,191.2 560.0,213.6 580.0,224.0 600.0,237.6" class="l" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"/><line x1="0" x2="600" y1="39.99999999999999" y2="39.99999999999999" class="lim" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"/></svg><span class="dv-lim" style="top:16.666666666666664%">One person's limit, $250,000</span><span class="dv-dot" id="dvDot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="dv-tip" id="dvTip" aria-hidden="true"></span></div><div class="dv-xa" aria-hidden="true"><span style="left:0%">1995</span><span style="left:33.33333333333333%">2005</span><span style="left:66.66666666666666%">2015</span><span style="left:100%">2025</span></div><p class="dv-xt" aria-hidden="true">Year the house was bought</p><table class="sr"><caption>Estimated gain by year bought</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Year bought</th><th scope="col">Estimated gain</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1995</td><td>$256,000</td></tr><tr><td>1996</td><td>$249,000</td></tr><tr><td>1997</td><td>$246,000</td></tr><tr><td>1998</td><td>$238,000</td></tr><tr><td>1999</td><td>$235,000</td></tr><tr><td>2000</td><td>$231,000</td></tr><tr><td>2001</td><td>$225,000</td></tr><tr><td>2002</td><td>$220,000</td></tr><tr><td>2003</td><td>$216,000</td></tr><tr><td>2004</td><td>$207,000</td></tr><tr><td>2005</td><td>$188,000</td></tr><tr><td>2006</td><td>$151,000</td></tr><tr><td>2007</td><td>$136,000</td></tr><tr><td>2008</td><td>$146,000</td></tr><tr><td>2009</td><td>$159,000</td></tr><tr><td>2010</td><td>$185,000</td></tr><tr><td>2011</td><td>$198,000</td></tr><tr><td>2012</td><td>$203,000</td></tr><tr><td>2013</td><td>$202,000</td></tr><tr><td>2014</td><td>$193,000</td></tr><tr><td>2015</td><td>$187,000</td></tr><tr><td>2016</td><td>$183,000</td></tr><tr><td>2017</td><td>$174,000</td></tr><tr><td>2018</td><td>$167,000</td></tr><tr><td>2019</td><td>$154,000</td></tr><tr><td>2020</td><td>$146,000</td></tr><tr><td>2021</td><td>$120,000</td></tr><tr><td>2022</td><td>$61,000</td></tr><tr><td>2023</td><td>$33,000</td></tr><tr><td>2024</td><td>$20,000</td></tr><tr><td>2025</td><td>$3,000</td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption>Our estimate from the federal house price index for the Myrtle Beach metro, applied to the county's middle home. Improvements and selling costs, not counted here, lower the gain.</figcaption><script>(function(){var d=[[1995,256000],[1996,249000],[1997,246000],[1998,238000],[1999,235000],[2000,231000],[2001,225000],[2002,220000],[2003,216000],[2004,207000],[2005,188000],[2006,151000],[2007,136000],[2008,146000],[2009,159000],[2010,185000],[2011,198000],[2012,203000],[2013,202000],[2014,193000],[2015,187000],[2016,183000],[2017,174000],[2018,167000],[2019,154000],[2020,146000],[2021,120000],[2022,61000],[2023,33000],[2024,20000],[2025,3000]],p=document.getElementById("dvPlot"),t=document.getElementById("dvTip"),o=document.getElementById("dvDot");function show(e){var b=p.getBoundingClientRect(),x=Math.min(Math.max((e.clientX-b.left)/b.width,0),1),i=Math.round(x*(d.length-1)),r=d[i],l=i/(d.length-1)*100,y=(1-r[1]/300000)*100;o.style.left=l+"%";o.style.top=y+"%";o.style.display="block";t.textContent="Bought in "+r[0]+": gain about $"+(Math.round(r[1]/1000)*1000).toLocaleString("en-US");t.style.left=Math.min(Math.max(l,18),82)+"%";t.style.top=Math.max(y-4,0)+"%";t.style.display="block"}p.addEventListener("pointermove",show);p.addEventListener("pointerdown",show);p.addEventListener("pointerleave",function(){t.style.display="none";o.style.display="none"})})()</script></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A typical house that sells for about $350,000 today would have cost about $167,000 in mid-2016, a gain of about $183,000. In mid-2006 it would have cost about $199,000, a gain of about $151,000. In mid-2021 it would have cost about $230,000, a gain of about $120,000.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">When you sell your main home, up to $250,000 of the gain is <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p523" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">free of federal income tax</a>. For a married couple filing a joint return, the limit is $500,000.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For the full amount, you must have owned the home for at least 2 of the 5 years before the sale. You must also have lived in it as your main home for 2 of those 5 years. You also must not have used the exclusion on another home in the 2 years before. On a joint return, one spouse can meet the ownership test, but both must meet the living-there test and the 2-year look-back for the $500,000.</p></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Before or after the divorce is final</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If you have no final decree of divorce or separate maintenance by the last day of your tax year, you <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p504" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">count as married</a> for that whole year. For most people that day is December 31, and the rule applies even if you live apart. Separate maintenance is a court order for support while a couple lives apart. Whether you can file a joint return, and so use the $500,000 limit, turns on that.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">When a couple sells a house they own together, each reports their own share of the gain. After the divorce, each single owner has their own $250,000 limit for their share, if they meet the tests.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">If one of you has moved out</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A spouse who moved out can still count the house as their home for the tax rule. That holds if a divorce decree, a written separation agreement or a support order lets the other spouse live there, and the other spouse does. Without one of those papers, the time after you move out does not count as time you lived there.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">A buyout and tax</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Generally, moving the house, or your share of it, to your spouse or ex-spouse as part of the divorce settlement is not a sale for tax purposes. There is no gain or loss to report. This does not hold if the spouse who receives it is a nonresident alien.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The spouse who keeps the house takes over its old cost basis, which is what was paid plus improvements. They can count the time the other spouse owned it, but must meet the 2-year living-there test on their own. A later sale then counts the gain from the original purchase.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If you sell before you meet the 2-year tests, a divorce is one of the listed reasons that can still allow a smaller, partial exclusion. Our <a href="/sell/capital-gains/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">capital gains page</a> has the full rules. Ask a tax professional before the terms of your agreement are set.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="steps"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The steps">What are the steps to sell a house in a divorce in South Carolina?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Seven steps, from living apart to the money from the sale. The order of steps 5 and 6 depends on when the two of you agree.</p><div class="dv-stepsrow"><ol class="dv-steps" aria-label="The steps to sell or keep a house in a South Carolina divorce, in order"><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">1</span><span class="c"><span class="t">Before the case</span><b>Living apart</b>The no-fault ground for divorce in South Carolina is <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=20&amp;chapter=3" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">one year of living apart</a>, without living together as a couple.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">2</span><span class="c"><span class="t">The case</span><b>The case is filed in Family Court</b>The <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=63&amp;chapter=3" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Family Court</a> decides divorce cases and who gets which property, the house included. Horry County's <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/departments/clerk-of-court/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Clerk of Court</a> for the Circuit and Family Court has its main office at 1301 Second Avenue in Conway. It has a second office at 332 Hinson Drive in Myrtle Beach.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">3</span><span class="c"><span class="t">If needed</span><b>A notice of the case on the house</b>Either spouse can file it with the clerk of court. It matters most when the house is in one spouse's name.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">4</span><span class="c"><span class="t">Before a judge decides</span><b>Mediation</b>Most contested issues, the house included, must go to mediation unless an exception applies.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">5</span><span class="c"><span class="t">The decision</span><b>Agreement or order</b>A signed agreement or the judge's order says if the house is sold, bought out or kept. That part of the order is final.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">6</span><span class="c"><span class="t">Before or after the divorce</span><b>The sale or the deed</b>The house can be sold before or after the divorce is final. On most grounds a final divorce cannot be granted until three months after the case is filed. On the one-year-apart ground, the divorce can be granted once the other spouse has answered or is in default, whichever comes first.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">7</span><span class="c"><span class="t">Closing</span><b>The money</b>A lawyer who holds the money from a sale, such as the closing attorney, <a href="https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/appellate/rule-407/rule-115/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">must keep any part the two of you dispute</a> separate. It stays separate until you agree, a court decides, or a narrow exception in the rule applies. The part you agree on is paid out promptly.</span></li></ol><div class="c3-aerial"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/hoa-liens-and-foreclosure/horry-government-justice-center-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/hoa-liens-and-foreclosure/horry-government-justice-center-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/hoa-liens-and-foreclosure/horry-government-justice-center-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="675" alt="The Horry County Government and Justice Center in Conway: brick wings on each side of a white columned entrance, seen across the parking lot" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: Pollinator at English Wikipedia. License: CC BY-SA 3.0" title="License: CC BY-SA 3.0">Photo: Pollinator at English Wikipedia</a></span></div><figcaption>Horry County's courthouse and county office complex in Conway.</figcaption></figure></div></div><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">A hearing, a mediation or a move to plan around?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send us your dates. We plan the listing and the closing around them, with both of you told at each step.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Send us your dates</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="selling-a-house-in-a-divorce-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Selling a house in a divorce: FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is South Carolina a 50/50 divorce state?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. South Carolina law has no equal-split rule. The judge weighs 15 factors to divide marital property, and the split is not always half.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Where does the money from the sale go until the divorce is settled?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The money is paid as your agreement or the court's order says. If you disagree about part of it, the lawyer holding it keeps that part separate until you agree or a court decides. The rule has one narrow exception.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do we need an appraisal for the divorce?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Not always. The two of you can agree on a value, from an appraisal or an agent's written price estimate, which may not be called an appraisal. If you do not agree, the judge decides the value and may appoint an expert, such as an appraiser.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Who pays the mortgage during the divorce?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Both of you still owe the lender while both names are on the loan. Who makes the payments while the case is open is a question for a family law attorney.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is a house I inherited marital property in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Usually not. What one spouse inherited is not marital property under the law. Whether it became marital, for example after it was put in both names, is a question for a family law attorney.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=20&amp;chapter=3" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 20-3</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=63&amp;chapter=3" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 63-3</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=15&amp;chapter=11" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 15-11</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=27&amp;chapter=7" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-7</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=12&amp;chapter=24" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-24</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=40&amp;chapter=57" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 40-57</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=40&amp;chapter=60" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 40-60</a>, <a href="https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/adr/rule-3/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC ADR Rule 3</a>, <a href="https://www.sccourts.org/resources/judicial-community/court-rules/appellate/rule-407/rule-115/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC RPC 1.15</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b2-1.3-02/limited-cash-out-refinance-transactions" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B2-1.3-02</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b3-6-05/monthly-debt-obligations" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B3-6-05</a>, <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/12/1701j-3" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">12 USC 1701j-3</a>, <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-I/part-191/section-191.5" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">12 CFR 191.5</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p523" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Pub. 523</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p504" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Pub. 504</a>, <a href="https://www.fhfa.gov/data/hpi" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FHFA HPI</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/departments/clerk-of-court/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Clerk of Court</a>, <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeDocType" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">County deeds</a>. Our reading of county deeds: the middle home. The gain chart: our estimate from the FHFA index.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Legal notice.</strong> Chapter3 Realty Corp is a licensed South Carolina real estate brokerage. We are not attorneys or tax professionals, and nothing on this page is legal or tax advice. For advice on your own divorce, talk to a South Carolina family law attorney; for the tax on your sale, a tax professional.</p><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Horry County's courthouse and county office complex in Conway. Photo: Pollinator at English Wikipedia, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY-SA 3.0</a>, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Horry_County_Courthouse_and_county_office_complex,_Conway,_South_Carolina_(18_November_2006).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikimedia Commons</a>. Chapter3 Realty cropped and resized the photo.</div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-a/hoa-liens-and-foreclosure/horry-government-justice-center-1200w.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-a/hoa-liens-and-foreclosure/horry-government-justice-center-1200w.webp","width":1200,"height":675,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","name":"Horry County's courthouse and county office complex in Conway","caption":"Horry County's courthouse and county office complex in Conway.","description":"The Horry County Government and Justice Center in Conway: brick wings on each side of a white columned entrance, seen across the parking lot","creator":{"@type":"Person","name":"Pollinator at English Wikipedia"},"creditText":"Pollinator at English Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","copyrightNotice":"© Pollinator at English Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","acquireLicensePage":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Horry_County_Courthouse_and_county_office_complex,_Conway,_South_Carolina_(18_November_2006).jpg","isBasedOn":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Horry_County_Courthouse_and_county_office_complex,_Conway,_South_Carolina_(18_November_2006).jpg","contentLocation":{"@type":"Place","name":"Horry County Government and Justice Center, 1301 Second Avenue, Conway, South Carolina"},"representativeOfPage":true}]}</script></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Selling the house in a divorce?</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.75);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:1.6rem">We price it, list it with both owners and keep every step in writing. Tell us where things stand, alone or together.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Have us talk with you about the house</a></div></section></main>
